title: Input history
tags: GUI
kind: guide
aliases: input-history recall ctrl-p ctrl-n ctrl-r history-commands
summary: Each text zone remembers what was submitted in it, per repository: history:prev and history:next walk back and forth, history:search searches.

The text zones keep what was submitted in them, ''per repository and per zone''.
Eight zones have a history:

* the command input in command mode, and in bash mode — two separate histories ([[The command input]], [[Bash mode]]) — the line of the [[shell panel]] sharing the bash mode's;
* the finder, the simplified query, the normal query and the columns of `metarecord-list`, the columns as the field shows them once applied;
* the search box of the [[help panel]];
* the intents file of the [[sync panel]], recorded when a plan is asked for.

!! Recalling

|!Command |!Does |
|<<key "history:prev self-focus" "Ctrl+P">> <<cmd "history:prev">> / <<key "history:next self-focus" "Ctrl+N">> <<cmd "history:next">> |the previous / the next entry, as in a terminal. What was being typed is kept at the newest end and comes back when walking past it. |
|<<key "history:search self-focus" "Ctrl+R">> <<cmd "history:search">> |a search box over the entries: newest first, each shown once, narrowed by the pieces typed, in order. `ctrl+p` / `ctrl+n` or the arrows choose, `Enter` puts the entry in the zone, `Escape` — or clicking elsewhere — gives up. These keys are the box's own. |

Editing the recalled text ends the walk: the edited text is the new draft. Leaving
the zone ends it too, so the first <<cmd "history:prev">> after coming back recalls
the newest entry again. A newest entry the zone already holds is skipped: after
applying a query that stays in its field, the first recall is the query before.

!! Which field

The argument is ''required'': the field the command acts on.

* `self-focus` is the field that has the focus: it is what the keys run. Where the focused field has no history, a key bound to it passes through as if it were not bound: outside a text field, `Ctrl+R` refreshes ([[Keybindings]]).
* A name is that field of the focused panel, which then takes the focus — `finder`, `simplified`, `normal`, `columns`, `search` for the help panel, `intents` for the sync panel, or `line` for the [[shell panel]]. `history:search finder` typed in the command input opens the finder's search.
* `self` is the focused panel's history: see below. It is what the keys run outside a text field. `history:search` has no such form.

Typed in the command input, `self-focus` finds nothing: the input gives up the focus
before the command runs, and no field holds it then. A field without history, or a
name the focused panel lacks, is an error.

!! A panel's history

Outside a text field <<key "history:prev self" "Ctrl+P">> and
<<key "history:next self" "Ctrl+N">> walk the panel's own history: the list of its
fields, in the order they were submitted. Each step runs <<cmd "history:prev">> on
the field at that place of the list, then the one before, and so on, whichever field
it is; <<cmd "history:next">> takes the same steps back. Going back through what was
tried in the finder and the query so needs no going to either: ''no field takes the
focus''.

A step only fills the field, as a recall in it does: the finder filters as it
changes, the query waits to be applied. A submitted field joins the list only if its
history gained an entry, so that each step has one to recall; a name the panel no
longer has is passed over. Editing a field, submitting one or leaving one ends the
walk. A panel without fields with a history lets the keys pass through.

!! What is recorded

An entry is recorded when the zone is ''submitted'' — `Enter`, or the command that
applies it — never while typing. The command mode records what actually ran: the
command chosen in the list, not the few letters typed to reach it. An answer to a
question — a command's argument, a script's prompt — is not recorded. Nor is a query
a command shows in the normal query field, a folder's for instance: it is not what
was typed there, and would bury it. Editing it and applying records the edit.

Submitting the same text twice in a row records it once. Each zone keeps its 1000
newest entries.

In a workspace without a repository the history still works, but lasts the session
only; it is not carried into a repository's history when one is opened.

!! Where it is kept

In the repository: one plain-text file per zone,
`.metafolder/gui/history/<zone>` — `shell:command`, `shell:bash`,
`metarecord-list:finder`, `metarecord-list:query`, `metarecord-list:normal`, `metarecord-list:columns`, `help:search`, `sync:intents` — one entry per line, the newest
last. A panel's list of fields is a file of the same kind, `metarecord-list:fields`,
`shell:fields`, `help:fields` or `sync:fields`, with one field's name per line, a name repeated when the field was
submitted twice in a row. The file is replaced whole at each addition (written aside, then renamed), so
an interrupted write leaves the previous history intact.

These files are ordinary content of `.metafolder/`, unlike `internal/`: they can be
tracked, and are in practice ignored by the `.metafolder` pattern every repository
starts with ([[Repository layout on disk]]).

The GUI reads and writes them itself; the daemon has no part in it and no endpoint
for it. It finds the repository's `.metafolder/` from `GET /repos`, which is right
also when that directory lives outside the root. A panel reaches its own zones
through `metafolder.history.read`, `metafolder.history.append` and
`metafolder.history.appendField`, and the commands
through `attachHistory` of `/__history.js`, which leaves on the field the handle they
call (`historyOf`) — on the element, because the shell and the panels each load
their own copy of the module. A zone's name is made of lowercase
letters, digits, `:`, `_` and `-`, 64 at most, so it cannot name a file outside the
history directory.
